%S A399819 30,4,8,4,1,6,8,1,4,2,12,2,9,6,2,2,5,8,1,6,4,2,10,1,1,2,4,1,4,4,3,5,6,
%T A399819 4,3,2,3,9,2,2,4,6,1,1,9,3,4,1,1,9,1,6,1,2,8,8,1,4,3,1,2,5,4,1,1,2,1,
%U A399819 6,1,3,2,2,9,2,1,1,2,4,1,2,5,2,1,4,4,7
%N A399819 Number of primes in the n-th maximal cluster of primes with consecutive gaps at most 8.
%C A399819 Whether a later cluster contains >=30 primes remains unresolved here. The Hardy-Littlewood prime-tuples conjecture predicts later clusters containing at least 31 primes, based on an admissible pattern with gaps at most 8.
%C A399819 For jump limits 2, 4 and 6, the initial counts 4, 9 and 24 are provably unique maxima since every later cluster contains at most 2, 6 and 19 primes, respectively.
%C A399819 For limit 12, the initial count 30 is exceeded by the cluster 127 to 293, containing 32 primes.
%H A399819 Jwalin Bhatt, <a href="/A399819/b399819.txt">Table of n, a(n) for n = 1..10000</a>
%e A399819 The first cluster is 2,...,113 and contains 30 primes, so a(1)=30. The next two clusters are {127,131,137,139} and {149,151,157,163,167,173,179,181}, giving a(2)=4 and a(3)=8. The fifth cluster is {211}, so a(5)=1.
%o A399819 (Python)
%o A399819 from sympy import nextprime
%o A399819 from itertools import islice
%o A399819 def gen_A399819():
%o A399819     p, count = 2, 1
%o A399819     while True:
%o A399819         q = int(nextprime(p))
%o A399819         if q - p > 8:
%o A399819             yield count
%o A399819             count = 0
%o A399819         count += 1
%o A399819         p = q
%o A399819 print(list(islice(gen_A399819(), 86)))
